int you saw these numbers  with a massively lopsided lead for  Hillary Clinton in 2020 as of right now  the super-delegates can't vote on the  first round super-delegates are widely  known to support establishment centrist  candidates so it is likely it's not  guaranteed but it's likely that if  nobody gets to 1991 delegates adding the  super-delegates for a second round of  voting would likely hand the convention  to an establishment centrist it's  unlikely that if you allow  super-delegates in they would go  overwhelmingly for Bernie Sanders or for  Elizabeth Warren so now it's very early  right so why on earth would we now be  talking about a brokered convention  we've only had two primaries Iowa and  New Hampshire that's very true but the  primaries so far  combined with the polling start to point  us towards a contested or brokered  convention again unless something  radically changes so this is about odds  there's no way to say right now we will  or we won't have a brokered convention  there's not even a way to say that we  could be 80 or even 90 percent certain  one way or the other but with every  passing day the polling is split among  so many candidates with every primary  we're three four or five candidates get  some delegates with the addition of  likely delegates for Tom Styer in South  Carolina and Michael Bloomberg in some  of the Super Tuesday States the odds of  a brokered convention continue to go up  and the really important element to  understand to all of this from a  strategic perspective is that this is  asymmetrical if the goal is to prevent a  specific candidate from securing the  nomination then you have an asymmetrical  situation where Bernie needs to win on  the first round but anybody who doesn't  want Bernie merely needs to deny Bernie  9 1991 delegates I've used the football  analogy before if you're winning by  seven points the offense needs a  touchdown  you only need to prevent a touchdown you  can get them all the way down the field  you can allow a field goal you can doall  as long as you prevent the touchdown you  win and thus you have an asymmetrical  situation it's very similar with Bernie  Sanders Bernie must get 50% plus one of  the delegates to get the nomination  because on the second round he is very  unlikely to do it the DNC insiders the  establishment merely have to prevent  Bernie from getting 50% plus one that  stacks things against Bernie Sanders  mathematically okay  case in point look at the five thirty  eight.com estimates right now as of  right now five thirty eight estimates  that Bernie Sanders has the 52 percent  chance of winning the most delegates  before the convention a plurality of  Delegates more delegates than any other  single candidate but only a 36 percent  chance of winning more than half this  would deny Bernie the nomination while  Bernie would still have a plurality of  the delegates so now let's look at the  math of this kind of at the big picture  level we will use our pool of three  thousand nine hundred and eighty pledged  delegates thirty nine eighty remember  you need half of that plus one that's  nineteen ninety plus one if the race  remains roughly the way it looks right  now from the national polls through the  primaries this is just our first  estimate you would expect for example  Bernie Sanders polling twenty three  point six nationally that would be  equivalent to roughly nine hundred and  thirty nine delegates Biden would get  seven sixty five Bloomberg five 65 war  and four ninety four and so on down now  in this scenario Bernie gets the most  delegates a plurality but it's not a  majority and thus he doesn't get the  nomination now an astute viewer will  notice this is too crude an estimate  because remember that most primaries  have this 15 percent viability threshold  so if you don't get 15 percent in a  particular primary you don't get any  delegates so in New Hampshire Elizabeth  Warren got 9.2 percent of the vote but  she didn't get nine percent of the  delegates she got zero delegates because  her share of the vote didn't make her  viable so over time the voting in any  one state  consolidate in some states candidates  could end up with a higher share of the  delegates than their vote total for  example if you look at New Hampshire  Bernie won twenty five point seven  percent of the vote but he got thirty  seven percent of the delegates because  of that viability threshold Amy  Klobuchar got twenty percent of the vote  in New Hampshire but she got six  delegates that's twenty five percent of  the delegates the non viable candidates  end up sort of subsidizing a higher  share of the delegates to the candidates  that do meet that 15% viability  threshold so this is a crude estimate  but I think you get the idea  with every primary we can fine-tune our  mathematical expectations in Iowa nobody  got more than thirty percent of the vote  and none of the delegate recipients  dropped out in New Hampshire no  candidate got more than 26 percent of  the vote and none of the delegate  recipients dropped out or are likely to  drop out anytime soon and remember that  this is self reinforcing the more it  looks like nobody will get to fifteen  fifty percent of the delegates the more  other candidates are incentivized to  stay in to see what can they bargain for  when we get to the convention that  possibility becomes more appealing to  other candidates when it doesn't seem  like anyone's going to get 50 percent  plus one this could actually get  significantly more split than it is now  making a brokered convention even more  likely because remember Tom Stiers  polling like 18 in a new poll in South  Carolina he's likely to be another  delegate recipient that would again  split the vote the delegates further  Michael Bloomberg despite the racist  audio that our friend Ben Dixon released  is polling in the mid to high-teens in  Florida and Texas which would be another  candidate getting delegates so this is  the math of how a brokered convention  even at this early stage seems to be  developing and it is asymmetrical  warfare in that if you deny Bernie the  nomination then it's wide open for the  establishment to pick whoever they want  in the second round of voting at the  convention so we'll of course be  following it will update the math with  each passing  primary but this is conceptually why  it's already something that's being  discussed one of our sponsors is privacy  comm they're giving you $5.00 when you  sign up for their completely free  service at privacy comm slash Pakman  I've been using privacy for a little  over a year now you've heard me talk  about it before it's a lifesaver and  here's how it works takes just a couple  of minutes to set up anytime you buy  something online or on the phone instead  of actually using your real credit card  number the privacy app and the browser  plugin let you give each company a  randomized virtual credit card number  that you create out of thin air it'll  even autofill the card number with one  click and the payment is taken out of  your checking account without the  merchant ever knowing your real  information so this allows you to keep  your banking information secure but also  to take control of your finances you can  create up to 12 of these virtual credit  cards a month you can set spending  limits you can freeze them you can  delete them any time you want so when  you do this it means you're not going to  be charged when you don't want to be  because you can destroy the virtual card  number right after using it which for  instance I love using free trials  because I know I won't be charged when  the trial is over if I use a virtual  credit card number you're keeping your  identity private by not telling  companies who you are you're keeping  your bank or credit card info protected  against data breaches and identity theft  and it's free there's no catch  whatsoever but if you want privacy also  offers a ten dollar 